{"name":"no method for the argument types","cells":["1 + \"a\""],"want":"MethodError: no method matching +(::Int64, ::String)\nThe function `+` exists, but no method is defined for this combination of argument types.\n\nClosest candidates are:\n  +(::Any, ::Any, !Matched::Any, !Matched::Any...)\n   @ Base operators.jl:642\n  +(::Number, !Matched::LinearAlgebra.UniformScaling)\n   @ LinearAlgebra ~/.pkgx/julialang.org/v1.12.6/share/julia/stdlib/v1.12/LinearAlgebra/src/uniformscaling.jl:148\n  +(::Number, !Matched::Missing)\n   @ Base missing.jl:124\n  ...\n"}
{"name":"a function with no methods","cells":["function nomethods_a end","nomethods_a(1)"],"want":"MethodError: no method matching nomethods_a(::Int64)\nThe function `nomethods_a` exists, but no method is defined for this combination of argument types."}
{"name":"an ambiguity","cells":["amb_a(x::Int, y) = 1","amb_a(x, y::Int) = 2","amb_a(1, 1)"],"want":"MethodError: amb_a(::Int64, ::Int64) is ambiguous.\n\nCandidates:\n  amb_a(x, y::Int64)\n    @ Main none:1\n  amb_a(x::Int64, y)\n    @ Main none:1\n\nPossible fix, define\n  amb_a(::Int64, ::Int64)\n"}
{"name":"a keyword argument that does not exist","cells":["kw_a(x; a=1) = x","kw_a(1; b=2)"],"want":"MethodError: no method matching kw_a(::Int64; b::Int64)\nThis method does not support all of the given keyword arguments (and may not support any).\n\nClosest candidates are:\n  kw_a(::Any; a) got unsupported keyword argument \"b\"\n   @ Main none:1\n"}
{"name":"too few arguments","cells":["few_a(x::Int, y::Int) = x","few_a(1)"],"want":"MethodError: no method matching few_a(::Int64)\nThe function `few_a` exists, but no method is defined for this combination of argument types.\n\nClosest candidates are:\n  few_a(::Int64, !Matched::Int64)\n   @ Main none:1\n"}
{"name":"an argument of the wrong type","cells":["typed_a(x::Int) = x","typed_a(\"s\")"],"want":"MethodError: no method matching typed_a(::String)\nThe function `typed_a` exists, but no method is defined for this combination of argument types.\n\nClosest candidates are:\n  typed_a(!Matched::Int64)\n   @ Main none:1\n"}
{"name":"several candidates","cells":["sev_a(x::Int) = 1","sev_a(x::String) = 2","sev_a(x::Int, y::Int) = 3","sev_a(1.5)"],"want":"MethodError: no method matching sev_a(::Float64)\nThe function `sev_a` exists, but no method is defined for this combination of argument types.\n\nClosest candidates are:\n  sev_a(!Matched::Int64, !Matched::Int64)\n   @ Main none:1\n  sev_a(!Matched::String)\n   @ Main none:1\n  sev_a(!Matched::Int64)\n   @ Main none:1\n"}
{"name":"a constructor","cells":["struct Pa; x::Int; end","Pa(\"a\")"],"want":"MethodError: Cannot `convert` an object of type String to an object of type Int64\nThe function `convert` exists, but no method is defined for this combination of argument types.\n\nClosest candidates are:\n  convert(::Type{T}, !Matched::T) where T<:Number\n   @ Base number.jl:6\n  convert(::Type{T}, !Matched::Number) where T<:Number\n   @ Base number.jl:7\n  convert(::Type{T}, !Matched::T) where T\n   @ Base Base_compiler.jl:133\n  ...\n"}
{"name":"calling a value that is no function","cells":["nf_a = 1","nf_a(2)"],"want":"MethodError: objects of type Int64 are not callable\nThe object of type `Int64` exists, but no method is defined for this combination of argument types when trying to treat it as a callable object.\nMaybe you forgot to use an operator such as *, ^, %, / etc. ?"}
{"name":"a call of a method with type parameters","cells":["par_a(x::Vector{T}) where T<:Integer = x","par_a([1.5])"],"want":"MethodError: no method matching par_a(::Vector{Float64})\nThe function `par_a` exists, but no method is defined for this combination of argument types.\n\nClosest candidates are:\n  par_a(!Matched::Vector{T}) where T<:Integer\n   @ Main none:1\n"}
{"name":"the world of the call","cells":["wa_f() = 1","wa_w = Base.get_world_counter()","wa_g(x::Int) = x","Base.invoke_in_world(wa_w, wa_g, 1)"],"want":"MethodError: no method matching wa_g(::Int64)\nThe applicable method may be too new: running in world age 38693, while current world is 38695.\n\nClosest candidates are:\n  wa_g(::Int64) (method too new to be called from this world context.)\n   @ Main none:1\n"}
